%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F78DB73C] ## Discretization error is a property of the problem and the method
%[text] As with all numerical methods, the first step is to decide how a problem will be discretized. We can break this down into two parts: discretizing the domain of the problem and discretizing the partial differential equation. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A87C9CD6] ### Discretizing the variables to create a mesh on the domain
%[text] Each independent variable in the problem must be subdivided into a discrete set of points. Combining all of these possible values generates a mesh covering the domain. Many problems will involve a bounded physical domain and a half-infinite time domain, $t\>0${"altText":"t\>0"}, on which to solve. A boundary value, $t\_{\\text{End}}${"altText":"t\_(End)"}, must be chosen to discretize the domain. The initial data will contain information about known values of the dependent variable on the boundary of this mesh. 
%[text] Different numerical methods operate on different forms of mesh, but all of them have to start with some form of discretization.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AF3F8723] ### Discretizing the equation using approximations of the derivatives
%[text] The other aspect of a numerical method is discretizing the partial differential equation itself to create a solvable system of equations. The method of finite differences approaches the problem by numerically approximating each derivative and using relationships between values on the mesh to determine systems of linear equations that can be solved for the values of the dependent variable.
%[text] The method of finite elements discretizes the space of possible solutions into localized test functions that can also be used to create a system of linear equations. In this case, the system of linear equations calculates the coefficients of each test function in the overall solution to the problem. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_37AD4637] ## Implement finite difference methods
%[text] If we try to explore problem $\\mathcal{P}${"altText":"P"}, we will quickly run into visualization challenges, such as the fact that the domain of the solution is 4-dimensional, and repetition, such as the similarity in $x${"altText":"x"}, $y${"altText":"y"}, and $z${"altText":"z"} that add length to the problem without offering new learning. If we simplify the [3-dimensional heat equation $(\\mathcal{P})${"altText":"(P)"}](internal:M_FA31F539) into a one-dimensional heat equation modeling heat in a uniform rod of length $L${"altText":"L"} with no internally generated heat, this will reduce the solution domain to being a 2-dimensional rectangle and leave a single first-order partial derivative and a single second order partial derivative. This becomes 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_15B9D537]  $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} = \\gamma \\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t) = γ (∂^2u)\/(∂ x^2)"},        $(\\mathcal{H})${"altText":"(H)"}
%[text] where $\\gamma = \\frac{k}{\\rho C\_p} \> 0${"altText":"γ = (k)\/(ρ C\_p) \> 0"} is the thermal diffusivity. Because this equation is first-order in time, we will need information about the value of the function at a particular time, $u(t\_0,x) = f(x)${"altText":"u(t\_0,x) = f(x)"}. However, the equation is second order in space, so we will need to know the Dirichlet boundary conditions, $u(t,0) = \\alpha(t)${"altText":"u(t,0) = α(t)"} and $u(t,L) = \\beta(t)${"altText":"u(t,L) = β(t)"}, or we will need to know the Neumann boundary conditions, $u\_x(t,0) = \\alpha(t)${"altText":"u\_x(t,0) = α(t)"} and $u\_x(t,0) = \\beta(t)${"altText":"u\_x(t,0) = β(t)"} where $u\_x = \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}${"altText":"u\_x = (∂ u)\/(∂ x)"}, or some combination to have a well-defined problem.  
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C96AD828] ### Example: Explicit Method
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5E2E11E4] #### Step 1: Discretize the problem
%[text] Let's use a constant rectangular mesh discretization. This means we must choose a step size $\\Delta t${"altText":"Δ t"}, to move forward in time and a step size $\\Delta x = \\frac{L}{n}${"altText":"Δ x = (L)\/(n)"} dividing the spatial domain into $n${"altText":"n"} intervals. Now our domain looks like this:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A rectangular mesh where the vertical steps are labeled Delta t and the larger horizontal steps are labeled Delta x. The left-hand, bottom, and right-hand edges of the mesh are highlighted in red.](text:image:5088)
%[text] Note that we know the value of our solution, $u(t,x)${"altText":"u(t,x)"}, around three edges of the domain: $u(t\_0,x) = f(x)${"altText":"u(t\_0,x) = f(x)"}, $u(t,0) = \\alpha(t)${"altText":"u(t,0) = α(t)"}, and $u(t,L) = \\beta(t)${"altText":"u(t,L) = β(t)"}. That leaves a mesh of interior values that we want to solve for. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A rectangular mesh where the values on the left are labeled alpha(j \* Delta t), the right hand points are labeled beta(j \* Delta t) and the bottom points are labeled f(k \* L / n). The interior mesh points are marked ?. The lower left point is labeled u(0,0) and the lower right point is labeled u(0,L).](text:image:980d)
%[text] For ease of notation, let's denote the point $u(t\_j,x\_m) = u\_{j,m}${"altText":"u(t\_j,x\_m) = u\_(j,m)"} where $t\_j = t\_0+j\\Delta t${"altText":"t\_j = t\_0+jΔ t"} and $x\_j = j\\Delta x${"altText":"x\_j = jΔ x"} where $j \\geq 0${"altText":"j ≥ 0"} and $m \\in \\{0,1,2,\\ldots,n\\}${"altText":"m in (0,1,2,…,n)"}. In this formulation, we know $u\_{0,m} = f\_m = f(x\_m)${"altText":"u\_(0,m) = f\_m = f(x\_m)"}, $u\_{j,0} = \\alpha\_j = \\alpha(t\_j)${"altText":"u\_(j,0) = α\_j = α(t\_j)"}, and $u\_{j,n} = \\beta\_j = \\beta(t\_j)${"altText":"u\_(j,n) = β\_j = β(t\_j)"}. The values that we do not know are $u\_{j,m}${"altText":"u\_(j,m)"} for $j \> 0${"altText":"j \> 0"} and $m \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,n-1\\}${"altText":"m in (1,2,…,n-1)"}. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_76D54D48] #### Step 2: Discretize the method
%[text] There are two derivatives in [equation $(\\mathcal{H})${"altText":"(H)"}](internal:M_15B9D537), so we must choose an approximation for $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t)"} and an approximation for $\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}${"altText":"(∂^2u)\/(∂ x^2)"}. There are many possible choices for each approximation. Still, each has tradeoffs between how complex it is to implement, how much error it will introduce, and whether the solution will be useful. 
%[text] A straightforward choice is to use a first-order forward difference approximation for the time derivative, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}(t\_j,x\_m) \\approx \\frac{u(t\_j+\\Delta t,x\_m)-u(t\_j,x\_m)}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{u\_{j+1,m}-u\_{j,m}}{\\Delta t}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t)(t\_j,x\_m) ≈ (u(t\_j+Δ t,x\_m)-u(t\_j,x\_m))\/(Δ t) = (u\_(j+1,m)-u\_(j,m))\/(Δ t)"}, 
%[text] on mesh points and for the spatial derivative, let's use a second-order centered difference approximation
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_915E8BEB] $\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}(t\_j,x\_m) \\approx \\frac{u(t\_j,x\_{m+1})-2u(t\_j,x\_m)+u(t\_j,x\_{m-1})}{(\\Delta x)^2} = \\frac{u\_{j,m+1}-2u\_{j,m}+u\_{j,m-1}}{(\\Delta x)^2}${"altText":"(∂^2u)\/(∂ x^2)(t\_j,x\_m) ≈ (u(t\_j,x\_(m+1))-2u(t\_j,x\_m)+u(t\_j,x\_(m-1)))\/((Δ x)^2) = (u\_(j,m+1)-2u\_(j,m)+u\_(j,m-1))\/((Δ x)^2)"}.
%[text] Plugging these into the differential equation, we find:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{u\_{j+1,m}-u\_{j,m}}{\\Delta t} = \\gamma\\left(\\frac{u\_{j,m+1}-2u\_{j,m}+u\_{j,m-1}}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\right)${"altText":"(u\_(j+1,m)-u\_(j,m))\/(Δ t) = γ((u\_(j,m+1)-2u\_(j,m)+u\_(j,m-1))\/((Δ x)^2))"}, 
%[text] which simplifies to
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_89C3E8C8] $u\_{j+1,m} =  u\_{j,m}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,m+1}-2u\_{j,m}+u\_{j,m-1}\\right).${"altText":"u\_(j+1,m) = u\_(j,m) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,m+1)-2u\_(j,m)+u\_(j,m-1))."} $(\\mathcal{E})${"altText":"(E)"}
%[text] Because we know all of the values $u\_{0,m} = f\_m${"altText":"u\_(0,m) = f\_m"}, we can estimate the values of $u\_{1,m}${"altText":"u\_(1,m)"} and then use those values to estimate $u\_{2,m}${"altText":"u\_(2,m)"} and so forth. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_56DFE612] #### Step 3: Check the stability of this method
%[text] A valid numerical approximation will eventually converge toward the solution. As you may remember from discussions of limiting behavior, however, not every method will converge. Some methods may bounce around in a bounded region around the solution, which may be sufficiently precise, while others may get farther and farther away no matter how small a step size is chosen.
%[text] One way to test the stability of a method is Fourier stability analysis. Suppose we assume that there is a Fourier series representation of the solution. In that case, each complex Fourier mode, $e^{ikx}${"altText":"e^(ikx)"}, is an eigenfunction of the differential equation with eigenvalue $\\lambda${"altText":"λ"} representing the scaling factor applied by each step forward in time.  This means that if $u(t,x) = e^{ikx}${"altText":"u(t,x) = e^(ikx)"} then $u(t+\\Delta t,x) = \\lambda e^{ikx}${"altText":"u(t+Δ t,x) = λ e^(ikx)"}.
%[text] Let $u\_{j,m} = u(t\_j,x\_m) = e^{ikx\_m}${"altText":"u\_(j,m) = u(t\_j,x\_m) = e^(ikx\_m)"}, where $k${"altText":"k"} is a real parameter selecting the mode. Plugging into our approximation, we have:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,m} =  u\_{j,m}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,m+1}-2u\_{j,m}+u\_{j,m-1}\\right)${"altText":"u\_(j+1,m) = u\_(j,m) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,m+1)-2u\_(j,m)+u\_(j,m-1))"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\lambda e^{ikx\_m}= e^{ikx\_m}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(e^{ikx\_{m+1}}-2e^{ikx\_m}+e^{ikx\_{m-1}}\\right)${"altText":"λ e^(ikx\_m)= e^(ikx\_m) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(e^(ikx\_(m+1))-2e^(ikx\_m)+e^(ikx\_(m-1)))"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $= e^{ikx\_m}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(e^{ik(x\_m+\\Delta x)}-2e^{ikx\_m}+e^{ik(x\_m-\\Delta x)}\\right)${"altText":"= e^(ikx\_m) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(e^(ik(x\_m+Δ x))-2e^(ikx\_m)+e^(ik(x\_m-Δ x)))"}
%[text] Because $e^{ikx\_m} \\neq 0${"altText":"e^(ikx\_m) ≠ 0"}, we can divide the entire equation by the Fourier mode, leaving us with:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\lambda = 1+ \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(e^{ik\\Delta x}-2+e^{-ik\\Delta x}\\right)${"altText":"λ = 1+ (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(e^(ikΔ x)-2+e^(-ikΔ x))"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $=1 - 2\\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(1- \\frac{e^{ik\\Delta x}+e^{-ik\\Delta x}}{2}\\right)${"altText":"=1 - 2(γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(1- (e^(ikΔ x)+e^(-ikΔ x))\/(2))"}.
%[text] Using trigonometric identities, we see that
%[text]{"align":"center"} $= 1-2\\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(1-\\cos(k\\Delta x)\\right)${"altText":"= 1-2(γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(1-cos(kΔ x))"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $=1-4\\frac{\\gamma\\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{k\\Delta x}{2}\\right)${"altText":"=1-4(γΔ t)\/((Δ x)^2)sin^2((kΔ x)\/(2))"}.              
%[text] Therefore, $|\\lambda| \\leq 1${"altText":"|λ| ≤ 1"} if 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_61540AAE] $-1 \\leq 1-4\\frac{\\gamma\\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{k\\Delta x}{2}\\right) \\leq 1${"altText":"-1 ≤ 1-4(γΔ t)\/((Δ x)^2)sin^2((kΔ x)\/(2)) ≤ 1"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $-2 \\leq-4\\frac{\\gamma\\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{k\\Delta x}{2}\\right) \\leq 0${"altText":"-2 ≤-4(γΔ t)\/((Δ x)^2)sin^2((kΔ x)\/(2)) ≤ 0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{1}{2} \\geq \\frac{\\gamma\\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{k\\Delta x}{2}\\right) \\geq 0${"altText":"(1)\/(2) ≥ (γΔ t)\/((Δ x)^2)sin^2((kΔ x)\/(2)) ≥ 0"}.
%[text] This gives us a relationship between the chosen step sizes $\\Delta t${"altText":"Δ t"} and $\\Delta x${"altText":"Δ x"}. As long as $\\frac{(\\Delta x)^2}{2\\gamma} \\geq \\Delta t${"altText":"((Δ x)^2)\/(2γ) ≥ Δ t"}, every eigenfunction in the Fourier series for $u${"altText":"u"} will be stable at each step of the estimate so the approximation will not diverge. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D145E7B0] #### Step 4: Formulate the linear algebra problem 
%[text] While it is possible to apply the method in [equation $(\\mathcal{E})${"altText":"(E)"}](internal:M_89C3E8C8) by hand, it is much more efficient to solve for all the values at each time step at once by expressing the relations as a linear system of equations. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,1} =  u\_{j,1}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,2}-2u\_{j,1}+u\_{j,0}\\right)${"altText":"u\_(j+1,1) = u\_(j,1) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,2)-2u\_(j,1)+u\_(j,0))"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,2} =  u\_{j,2}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,3}-2u\_{j,2}+u\_{j,1}\\right)${"altText":"u\_(j+1,2) = u\_(j,2) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,3)-2u\_(j,2)+u\_(j,1))"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,3} =  u\_{j,3}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,4}-2u\_{j,3}+u\_{j,2}\\right)${"altText":"u\_(j+1,3) = u\_(j,3) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,4)-2u\_(j,3)+u\_(j,2))"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vdots${"altText":"…"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,m} =  u\_{j,m}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,m+1}-2u\_{j,m}+u\_{j,m-1}\\right)${"altText":"u\_(j+1,m) = u\_(j,m) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,m+1)-2u\_(j,m)+u\_(j,m-1))"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vdots${"altText":"…"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,n-1} =  u\_{j,n-1}  + \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j,n}-2u\_{j,n-1}+u\_{j,n-2}\\right)${"altText":"u\_(j+1,n-1) = u\_(j,n-1) + (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j,n)-2u\_(j,n-1)+u\_(j,n-2))"}
%[text] Let $\\mu = \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}${"altText":"μ = (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)"} be the multiplier that appears repeatedly in these equations. If we create vectors $\\vec{u}^{(j+1)} = \\pmatrix{u\_{j+1,1} \\cr u\_{j+1,2} \\cr u\_{j+1,3} \\cr \\vdots \\cr u\_{j+1,n-2} \\cr u\_{j+1,n-1}}${"altText":"vector u^((j+1)) = matrix rows: u\_(j+1,1) ; u\_(j+1,2) ; u\_(j+1,3) ; … ; u\_(j+1,n-2) ; u\_(j+1,n-1)"}, where $\\vec{u}^{(0)} = \\pmatrix{f\_1 \\cr f\_2 \\cr f\_3 \\cr \\vdots \\cr f\_{n-2} \\cr f\_{n-1}}${"altText":"vector u^((0)) = matrix rows: f\_1 ; f\_2 ; f\_3 ; … ; f\_(n-2) ; f\_(n-1)"} and $\\vec{b}^{(j)} = \\pmatrix{ \\mu \\alpha\_j \\cr 0 \\cr 0 \\cr \\vdots \\cr 0 \\cr \\mu\\beta\_j}${"altText":"vector b^((j)) = matrix rows: μ α\_j ; 0 ; 0 ; … ; 0 ; μβ\_j"} encode the initial conditions, then the matrix $A = \\pmatrix{1-2\\mu & \\mu & & & &  \\cr \\mu & 1-2\\mu & \\mu & && \\cr & \\mu & 1-2\\mu & \\mu && \\cr &&\\mu&\\ddots & \\ddots & \\cr &&& \\ddots & \\ddots & \\mu \\cr &&&& \\mu & 1-2\\mu}${"altText":"A = matrix rows: 1-2μ , μ , , , , ; μ , 1-2μ , μ , , , ; , μ , 1-2μ , μ , , ; , , μ, … , … , ; , , , … , … , μ ; , , , , μ , 1-2μ"} relates the entire system.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_EAD0EF16] $\\vec{u}^{(j+1)} = A\\vec{u}^{(j)} + \\vec{b}^{(j)}${"altText":"vector u^((j+1)) = Avector u^((j)) + vector b^((j))"}.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8C5DD920] #### Step 5: How good is our estimate?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A778FA4B] The error analysis of derivative approximations shows us that 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}(t\_i,x\_j) = \\frac{u(t\_i+\\Delta t,x\_j)-u(t\_i,x\_j)}{\\Delta t} + \\mathcal{O}(\\Delta x)${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t)(t\_i,x\_j) = (u(t\_i+Δ t,x\_j)-u(t\_i,x\_j))\/(Δ t) + O(Δ x)"}, 
%[text] and
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}(t\_i,x\_j) = \\frac{u(t\_i,x\_{j+1})-2u(t\_i,x\_j)+u(t\_i,x\_{j-1})}{(\\Delta x)^2} + \\mathcal{O}\\left((\\Delta x)^2\\right)}${"altText":"(∂^2u)\/(∂ x^2)(t\_i,x\_j) = (u(t\_i,x\_(j+1))-2u(t\_i,x\_j)+u(t\_i,x\_(j-1)))\/((Δ x)^2) + O((Δ x)^2)"}.
%[text] To ensure the best results with a given step size $\\Delta x${"altText":"Δ x"}, we need the error contributions due to estimations on each side of the differential equation to be of comparable magnitude.  This means that 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathcal{O}(\\Delta t) \\approx \\mathcal{O}\\left((\\Delta x)^2\\right)${"altText":"O(Δ t) ≈ O((Δ x)^2)"}, 
%[text] or in other words, we have to choose
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\Delta t \\approx (\\Delta x)^2${"altText":"Δ t ≈ (Δ x)^2"}.
%[text] To use a finer resolution, say $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\Delta x}{10}${"altText":"(Δ x)\/(10)"}, in our spatial solution, we have to take much smaller time steps, say $\\frac{\\Delta t}{100}${"altText":"(Δ t)\/(100)"}. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F43FA524] ### Implement an explicit method
%[text] ![](text:image:9af7)**Exercise 2:**
%[text] Work through unit tests for the components of `ExplicitPDE.m`.
edit("ImplementExplicitSolverSoln.m")
%[text] Write the function `ExplicitPDE` to implement the solution method to 1D heat equations presented in [this example](internal:M_EAD0EF16).
edit("ExplicitPDESoln.m")
%%
%[text] Test your function by using it to solve a problem.
% Define the parameters and boundary functions for the problem
L = 4;                   % Length of the rod
gamma = 3;               % Diffusivity, gamma > 0
dx = 0.1;                % Spatial step size
tEnd = 0.15;             % The time at which to finish the approximation
f = @(x) x.^2-4.*x;      % u(0,x)
alpha = @(t) sin(100*t); % u(t,0)
% Using the piecewise function requires a symbolic function
syms betaFunc(t)
betaFunc(t) = piecewise((0 <= t) & (t < 0.5), ...
    t,(0.5 <= t) & (t <= 1),1-t,0);       % u(t,L)
% To ensure that the output of beta is a double
beta = @(t) double(betaFunc(t));

u = ExplicitPDESoln(f,alpha,beta,tEnd,dx,gamma,L);
%[text] Plot the initial value `u0` and the final value `u` on the same plot.
plot(0:dx:L,f(0:dx:L)) %[output:7758e5d2]
hold on %[output:7758e5d2]
plot(0:dx:L,[alpha(tEnd);u;beta(tEnd)]) %[output:7758e5d2]
hold off %[output:7758e5d2]
title("Solving $\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \gamma \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:7758e5d2]
subtitle("$t=$"+tEnd,"Interpreter","latex") %[output:7758e5d2]
xlabel("$x$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:7758e5d2]
ylabel("$u$","Interpreter","latex") %[output:7758e5d2]
legend(["u(0,x)" "u(t,x)"],"Location","north") %[output:7758e5d2]
%%
%[text] **Reference Output**
%[text] With $L=4${"altText":"L=4"}, $\\Delta x=0.1${"altText":"Δ x=0.1"}, and $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} = 3\\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t) = 3(∂^2u)\/(∂ x^2)"} with $u(0,x) = x^2-4x${"altText":"u(0,x) = x^2-4x"}, $u(t,0)=\\sin(100t)${"altText":"u(t,0)=sin(100t)"} and $u(t,4) = \\begin{cases} t & 0 \\leq t \< 0.5 \\\\ 1-t & 0.5 \\leq t \< 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{else} \\end{cases}${"altText":"u(t,4) = piecewise: t when 0 ≤ t \< 0.5 ; 1-t when 0.5 ≤ t \< 1 ; 0 when else"}, you should have the following results at $t=0.05${"altText":"t=0.05"}, $t= 0.1${"altText":"t= 0.1"}, $t=0.15${"altText":"t=0.15"} and $t=0.2${"altText":"t=0.2"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Screenshot of expected results at t=0.05](text:image:75e8) ![Screenshot of expected results at t=0.1](text:image:1f3c)
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Screenshot of expected results at t=0.15](text:image:8441) ![Screenshot of expected results at t=0.2](text:image:8c14)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_15eb] ### Example: Implicit Methods
%[text] As with numerical methods for ordinary differential equations, implicit solution methods for partial differential equations require solving systems of equations. Taking the same [1D heat equation $(\\mathcal{H})${"altText":"(H)"}](internal:M_15B9D537) and the [same discretization](internal:H_A87C9CD6) of the variables, as with the explicit method example, we can begin by discretizing the equation differently.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_440b] #### Step 1: Discretizing the equation using approximations of the derivatives
%[text] Rather than a forward difference approximation for $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t)"}, in this case, use a first-order backward difference equation,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}(t\_j,x\_m) \\approx \\frac{u(t\_j,x\_m)-u(t\_j-\\Delta t,x\_m)}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{u\_{j,m}-u\_{j-1,m}}{\\Delta t}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t)(t\_j,x\_m) ≈ (u(t\_j,x\_m)-u(t\_j-Δ t,x\_m))\/(Δ t) = (u\_(j,m)-u\_(j-1,m))\/(Δ t)"}, 
%[text] on mesh points. For the spatial derivative, let's use the same [second-order centered difference approximation](internal:M_915E8BEB) that we did in the explicit method example. Shifting values from $j${"altText":"j"} to $j+1${"altText":"j+1"} so we don't try to refer to $u\_{-1}${"altText":"u\_(-1)"} when $j=0${"altText":"j=0"}, and plugging these into the differential equation, we find:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{u\_{j+1,m}-u\_{j,m}}{\\Delta t} = \\gamma\\left(\\frac{u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1}}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\right)${"altText":"(u\_(j+1,m)-u\_(j,m))\/(Δ t) = γ((u\_(j+1,m+1)-2u\_(j+1,m)+u\_(j+1,m-1))\/((Δ x)^2))"}, 
%[text] which simplifies to
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_FF1F54C7] $u\_{j+1,m} -  \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1}\\right)=  u\_{j,m}${"altText":"u\_(j+1,m) - (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)(u\_(j+1,m+1)-2u\_(j+1,m)+u\_(j+1,m-1))= u\_(j,m)"}.   $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3323] #### Step 2: Check the stability of this method
%[text] ![](text:image:614f) **Exercise 3:** 
%[text] Follow the example of the [Fourier stability analysis of the explicit method](internal:H_56DFE612) $(\\mathcal{E})${"altText":"(E)"} to compute the Fourier stability of the implicit method $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}. 
%[text] **Solution:**
%[text] Let $u\_{j,m} = u(t\_j,x\_m) = e^{ikx\_m}${"altText":"u\_(j,m) = u(t\_j,x\_m) = e^(ikx\_m)"}, where $k${"altText":"k"} is a real parameter selecting the mode, let $u\_{j+1,m} = \\lambda e^{ikx\_m}${"altText":"u\_(j+1,m) = λ e^(ikx\_m)"}, and let $\\mu = \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}${"altText":"μ = (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2)"}, as before. Plugging into our approximation, we have:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_{j+1,m} -  \\mu\\left(u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1}\\right)=  u\_{j,m}${"altText":"u\_(j+1,m) - μ(u\_(j+1,m+1)-2u\_(j+1,m)+u\_(j+1,m-1))= u\_(j,m)"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\lambda e^{ikx\_m} - \\mu\\left(\\lambda e^{ikx\_{m+1}}-2\\lambda e^{ikx\_m}+\\lambda e^{ikx\_{m-1}}\\right)= e^{ikx\_m} ${"altText":"λ e^(ikx\_m) - μ(λ e^(ikx\_(m+1))-2λ e^(ikx\_m)+λ e^(ikx\_(m-1)))= e^(ikx\_m)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\lambda e^{ikx\_m}\\left(1-\\mu e^{ik\\Delta x}+2\\mu -\\mu e^{-ik\\Delta x}\\right)= e^{ikx\_m} ${"altText":"λ e^(ikx\_m)(1-μ e^(ikΔ x)+2μ -μ e^(-ikΔ x))= e^(ikx\_m)"} 
%[text] Because $e^{ikx\_m} \\neq 0${"altText":"e^(ikx\_m) ≠ 0"}, we can divide the entire equation by the Fourier mode, apply a trigonometric identity, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{e^{i\\theta}+e^{-i\\theta}}{2}${"altText":"cos(θ) = (e^(iθ)+e^(-iθ))\/(2)"},
%[text] and solve for $\\lambda${"altText":"λ"} leaving us with:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\lambda \\left(1+2\\mu-\\mu\\left(e^{ik\\Delta x} +e^{-ik\\Delta x}\\right)\\right)=1 ${"altText":"λ (1+2μ-μ(e^(ikΔ x) +e^(-ikΔ x)))=1"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{1+2\\mu-2\\mu \\cos(k\\Delta x)} ${"altText":"λ=(1)\/(1+2μ-2μ cos(kΔ x))"} 
%[text] Using another trigonometric identity, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1-\\cos(\\theta)}{2}${"altText":"sin^2((θ)\/(2)) = (1-cos(θ))\/(2)"},
%[text] we see that     
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{1+4\\mu\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{k\\Delta x}{2}\\right)} ${"altText":"λ=(1)\/(1+4μsin^2((kΔ x)\/(2)))"}.
%[text] Therefore, because $\\gamma\>0${"altText":"γ\>0"} and $\\Delta t\>0${"altText":"Δ t\>0"}, thus $\\mu = \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2} \> 0${"altText":"μ = (γ Δ t)\/((Δ x)^2) \> 0"}, we see that $|\\lambda| \\leq 1${"altText":"|λ| ≤ 1"} for all values of $\\Delta x${"altText":"Δ x"} and $\\Delta t${"altText":"Δ t"}.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_867b] #### Step 3: Formulate the linear algebra problem 
%[text] Since we have chosen to use the same discretization of the problem, we can use the same vector $\\vec{u}^{(j)} = \\pmatrix{u\_{j,1}\\cr \\vdots \\cr u\_{j,n-1}}${"altText":"vector u^((j)) = matrix rows: u\_(j,1); … ; u\_(j,n-1)"} as before. In terms of $\\vec{u}^{(j+1)}${"altText":"vector u^((j+1))"} and $\\vec{u}^{(j)}${"altText":"vector u^((j))"}, the [entire system of equations $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}](internal:M_FF1F54C7) relating times $t\_j${"altText":"t\_j"} and $t\_{j+1}${"altText":"t\_(j+1)"} are given by 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B\\vec{u}^{(j+1)} - \\vec{b}^{(j+1)} = \\vec{u}^{(j)}${"altText":"Bvector u^((j+1)) - vector b^((j+1)) = vector u^((j))"},
%[text] or in other words, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_56AE4F3A] $\\vec{u}^{(j+1)} = B^{-1}\\left(\\vec{u}^{(j)}+\\vec{b}^{(j+1)}\\right)${"altText":"vector u^((j+1)) = B^(-1)(vector u^((j))+vector b^((j+1)))"},
%[text] where $B = \\pmatrix{1+2\\mu & -\\mu & & & &  \\cr -\\mu & 1+2\\mu & -\\mu & && \\cr & -\\mu & 1+2\\mu & -\\mu && \\cr && -\\mu&\\ddots & \\ddots & \\cr &&& \\ddots & \\ddots & -\\mu \\cr &&&& -\\mu & 1+2\\mu}${"altText":"B = matrix rows: 1+2μ , -μ , , , , ; -μ , 1+2μ , -μ , , , ; , -μ , 1+2μ , -μ , , ; , , -μ, … , … , ; , , , … , … , -μ ; , , , , -μ , 1+2μ"} and $\\vec{b}^{(j+1)} = \\pmatrix{\\mu\\alpha\_{j+1}\\cr 0 \\cr 0 \\cr \\vdots \\cr 0 \\cr \\mu\\beta\_{j+1}}${"altText":"vector b^((j+1)) = matrix rows: μα\_(j+1); 0 ; 0 ; … ; 0 ; μβ\_(j+1)"}. 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_58c0] #### Step 4: How good is our estimate?
%[text] ![](text:image:5091) **Exercise 4.**
%[text] Follow the [error calculation](internal:H_8C5DD920) in the explicit method example to determine an appropriate relationship between `dt` and `dx` to control the derivative approximation error.
% Enter the correct relationship here

% %% Solution
dt = dx^2;
% %% End Solution
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0e84] ### Implement an implicit method
%[text] ![](text:image:3662) **Exercise 5.**
%[text] Implement the [implicit method $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}](internal:M_FF1F54C7). 
edit("ImplicitPDESoln.m")
