%[text] %[text:anchor:H_73BAA29C] ## Apply numerical methods
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8DC8EC32] ### Mix and match
%[text] While the Crank-Nicolson method has many desirable features among the three methods you have implemented, it also has some negatives. Specifically, while diffusive equations such as heat immediately smooth out corners, the Crank-Nicolson method preserves corners, as shown in the reference output. Even as the overall shape is smoothing, the point where the original function had a discontinuous derivative continues to appear in the numerical solution with a discontinuous derivative. Consider the following equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} = \\gamma \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x^2}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t) = γ (∂^2 u)\/(∂ x^2)"}  with boundary conditions $\\begin{cases} u(0,x) = f(x) & \\\\ u(t,0) = \\alpha(t) & \\\\ u(t,L) = \\beta(t) & \\end{cases}${"altText":"piecewise: u(0,x) = f(x) when ; u(t,0) = α(t) when ; u(t,L) = β(t) when"}
% Define the parameters and boundary functions for the problem
L = 4;                   % Length of the rod
gamma = 1;               % Diffusivity, gamma > 0
dx = 0.01;               % Spatial step size
tEnd = 0.08;             % The time at which to finish the approximation
syms fFunc(x)
fFunc(x) = piecewise(x<=1,2*x,(x>1)&(x<=2),5-3*x,... %[output:group:79164586] %[output:21a4f3e8]
    (x>2)&(x<=3),-1,(3<x),2*x-7) %[output:group:79164586] %[output:21a4f3e8]
%fFunc(x) = piecewise(x<0.2,-x,(0.2<=x)&(x<=0.7),x-0.4,0.7<x,1-x)
f = @(x) double(fFunc(x));      % u(0,x)
alpha = @(t) 0;                 % u(t,0)
beta = @(t) 1;                  % u(t,L)
plot(0:dx:L,f(0:dx:L)) %[output:5f767f85]
%[text] **Reference Output**
%[text] With $L=4${"altText":"L=4"}, $\\Delta x=0.1${"altText":"Δ x=0.1"}, and $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} = \\frac{\\partial^2u}{\\partial x^2}${"altText":"(∂ u)\/(∂ t) = (∂^2u)\/(∂ x^2)"} with $u(0,x) = \\begin{cases} 2x & x \\leq 1 \\\\ 5-3x & 1 \< x \\leq 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \< x \\leq 3 \\\\ 2x-7 & 3 \< x \\end{cases} ${"altText":"u(0,x) = piecewise: 2x when x ≤ 1 ; 5-3x when 1 \< x ≤ 2 ; -1 when 2 \< x ≤ 3 ; 2x-7 when 3 \< x"}, $u(t,0)=0${"altText":"u(t,0)=0"} and $u(t,4) = 1${"altText":"u(t,4) = 1"}, at $t=0.01${"altText":"t=0.01"} and $t=0.02${"altText":"t=0.02"} we have:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Screenshot showing Crank-Nicolson with corners at t=0.01](text:image:9e6e) ![Screenshot showing Crank-Nicolson with corners at t=0.02](text:image:9b2a)
%[text] ![](text:image:874a) **Try**. Can you reproduce this output? 
uCN = CNSoln(f,alpha,beta,tEnd,dx,gamma,L); %[output:24646a3e]
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3cfd) **Try**. What results do you find using the explicit solution method? 
figure %[output:3d854b5d]
uExp = ExplicitPDESoln(f,alpha,beta,tEnd,dx*100,gamma,L); %[output:3d854b5d]
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:0b06) **Try**. What results do you find using the implicit solution method? 
figure %[output:0d800865]
uImp = ImplicitPDESoln(f,alpha,beta,tEnd,dx*100,gamma,L); %[output:0d800865]
%%
%[text] As you can see, the Crank-Nicolson method generates smooth curves everywhere except close to points where the initial data had a corner. To observe both long and short timescales, it can be the most efficient to mix methods such as using a pure implicit method with a small timestep $\\Delta t \\approx (\\Delta x)^2${"altText":"Δ t ≈ (Δ x)^2"} for a short time and then switching to a comparable-error method with a longer potential timestep such as Crank-Nicolson with $\\Delta t\\approx \\Delta x${"altText":"Δ t≈ Δ x"} to observe longer-term behavior.
